From 9d224e8bcae1b9578689241088ce53acacfc2e32 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Guyot <pguyot@kallisys.net>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 08:01:39 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Store each map entry's hash beside it, and carry it from walk
 to lookup

A CHAMP node held [Key, Value] per entry and recomputed term_hash whenever it
needed one. Two places wanted it back: pushing a resident entry down a level on
a slot collision rehashed the key it was moving, and a walk that had just
produced a key knew nothing about it that a following lookup could use.

Entries are now [Hash, Key, Value]. The stored hash costs a word each and pays
for itself three ways: a slot collision moves an entry without rehashing it, a
lookup that lands on the wrong key rejects it by comparing hashes instead of
comparing terms, and iteration hands the hash out for free.

That last one is what the memo is for. Walking one map and looking each of its
keys up in another is the shape the compiler spends its map time in, and the
key's hash is already known from the first map; a one-entry, scheduler-thread
memo carries it to the second, where it would otherwise be recomputed over a
compound key. It is keyed on the key's term, so on a heap address for a boxed
key, and cannot outlive it: only the cursor writes it, so a memoized key always
belongs to the process this thread is running, and the collector drops it
whenever terms move while the scheduler drops it when this thread takes on a
different process.

The hash narrows from 32 bits to 28 so that it fits a small integer on a 32-bit
build, costing one level of depth. That makes fully-equal hashes likelier than
before, so the root now records the size of the largest collision node rather
than merely that one exists, and a put reserves for the node the map actually
holds instead of for the map.

